[about]
Title=8 - Build with an AI Assistant
Intro=One want: a new feature written with an AI assistant, in the kit's house style, and proved before anyone hears it. The skills that teach an assistant the rules, the three sentences that carry the kit into a chat, the method -- specify, build small, verify without sight -- and a planned misstep the check catches.
Setup=An app made with newHomerApp, as in walk three, and a command prompt in its folder.
Homework=Write accept dot inix for one feature you want, then ask an assistant for it and run check.

[step]

Say=One want: a feature written with an AI assistant that follows the house rules, and proved before anyone hears it. This walk leans on walks three and four: an app from newHomerApp, and build, check and release.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The kit teaches an assistant through its skills: folders of instructions, one for each part of the work -- code, dialogs, installers, tutorials. Before trusting them, the kit checks them as it checks code. I type the command.
Key=scripts\checkSkills
Hear=scripts\checkSkills

[step]

Say=Enter runs it.
Key=Enter
Hear=17 skills checked, 0 problems.

[step]

Say=Seventeen skills, none broken. A skill whose description held an angle bracket would be refused when uploaded, and this check names it first.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Next, the assistant has to find the kit. You work in your own project's folder, and an assistant started there knows nothing of HomerDev until something tells it. One button does it once, for every assistant on the computer: in the HomerDev dialog, Connect AI tools. Its key is Alt plus T.
Key=Alt+T
Hear=Connect AI tools
Hear=Connected 3 AI tools to the Homer Development Kit at C colon backslash Program Files backslash HomerDev.
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=What it did: Claude Code, Codex and Gemini each read a personal instructions file in every project. It added a short note to each, saying where the kit is and to read its AGENTS file first, and it linked the kit's skills into each assistant's own skills folder. Your own instructions in those files are left alone.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=From now on, mention Homer or HomerDev in any project, and say what you want in plain words. I start my assistant in the app's folder and type one line.
Key=
Hear=Using HomerDev, add a Prefix box to this dialog, the Homer way.

[step]

Say=The assistant reads the kit's instructions, then the skill for dialogs, and uses the kit's Lbc class rather than inventing its own. Without connecting, one sentence at the start of a session does the same.
Key=
Hear=Read the AGENTS file of the Homer Development Kit, in the folder the HomerDev environment variable names, and follow it.

[step]

Say=Then the method the kit is shaped around. Specify before you generate: write accept dot inix first, a few named checks of what done means, so the assistant works to your idea of done, not its own. I open it in EdSharp.
Key=Control+O
Hear=Open dialog
Hear=File name: edit
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Each check is a name and a command, and check runs them all. Now one change, then one build: the assistant has written the Prefix box, I put its file in place, and type build.
Key=build
Hear=build

[step]

Say=Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=Built PatternRename underscore setup dot e x e, version 1.0.1
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=The installer is built. Then check, which refuses what release would refuse, and writes the evidence.
Key=check
Hear=check

[step]

Say=Enter.
Key=Enter
Hear=13 checks passed, 0 checks failed, 2 checks not checked.

[step]

Say=The two not checked are the build and the smoke run, which come with dash dash build. A planned misstep: the assistant gave two controls captions that start with the same letter. I run check again.
Key=Enter
Hear=access letters -- Prefix: P is used by Pattern and Prefix
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=The check names the caption and the clash; the fix is a caption, not a key table. The assistant renames the box Text Before Each Name, so it takes T from the first letter of a word; I rebuild, and check passes again.
Key=Enter
Hear=13 checks passed, 0 checks failed, 2 checks not checked.

[step]

Say=Last, verify without sight: the log says what happened, and your ears do the rest. In the running program, Control plus F1 turns on the Key Describer, so the new box's key says what it does.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=Key Describer On

[step]

Say=Alt plus T, T for Text, the first letter of the new caption.
Key=Alt+T
Hear=Alt plus T, Text Before Each Name
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Control plus F1 again, and the keys act once more.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=No Key Describer

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I name the step; the reader gives the command.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Say what done means, first.
Key=
Hear=accept dot inix

[step]

Say=Prove it before release.
Key=
Hear=check

[step]

Say=Make sure the instructions an assistant reads are sound.
Key=
Hear=checkSkills
